WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:05.263
- You know, for a large
scale organizations, that--

00:00:05.263 --> 00:00:06.888
- Really quick, I apologize.

00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:07.889
- [Stephen] Yeah.

00:00:07.889 --> 00:00:09.118
- It is "Interrupted By."

00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:11.701
(upbeat music)

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.900
We have rebranded my podcast
to "Interrupted By Gary V."

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.460
So I apologize upfront if I interrupt.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.250
It's only out of sheer excitement

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.700
and trying to be as efficient
with our time as possible.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.950
I'm so exited you're on the show.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.307
Why don't you tell the
Vayner Nation who you are

00:00:36.307 --> 00:00:37.560
and a little bit about your career.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.339
And I'm obviously very curious to

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.520
how you thought about the book

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.520
and then obviously I'd
like to get your take

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.530
on current events and shoot
the shit here a little bit.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.919
- [Stephen] Sure.

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:51.332
- Thanks for being on.

00:00:51.332 --> 00:00:52.774
- Sure, I'm Steve Schwarzman,

00:00:52.774 --> 00:00:57.774
I'm professionally co
founder and chairman and CEO

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.900
of a company called Blackstone.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.711
Blackstone is the largest
company in the world

00:01:04.711 --> 00:01:09.313
that does what's called
alternative investing.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.750
That's a private equity where
we're one of the largest

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.880
in the world, I think we're the largest.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.460
Real estate, globally,
we're the largest owner of

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.030
real estate globally.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:23.920
Hedge funds,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.645
we're the largest allocator
to hedge funds in the world.

00:01:27.645 --> 00:01:32.645
Leverage credit, where we
extend loans and other types

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.820
of securities to people
who want to borrow money.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.689
- [Gary] Not the largest
in the world in that,

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:39.819
'cause you'd rather go there--

00:01:39.819 --> 00:01:40.981
- No, one of the top two.

00:01:40.981 --> 00:01:41.983
(laughter)

00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:42.999
- [Gary] Understood.

00:01:42.999 --> 00:01:44.004
Little more work on that one.

00:01:44.004 --> 00:01:45.019
Fair enough.

00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:46.054
(laughter)

00:01:46.054 --> 00:01:48.010
- [Stephen] We got more work to do.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.491
We do other things.

00:01:49.491 --> 00:01:54.080
Like invest in live
science and growth equity,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.308
which are newer type companies.

00:01:56.308 --> 00:01:59.140
And we grow very rapidly.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.140
We got about 550 billion dollars of assets

00:02:04.808 --> 00:02:09.170
which won't mean much to most listeners,

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:14.170
but it's a lot for sure.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.540
And we're one of the
most profitable companies

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.760
in money management in the world.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.430
We have what's called market value

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.540
which is what the stock market values as

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.430
at about 68 billion dollars.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.910
And I started the company with $400,000.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.310
So in the capitalist world--

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.350
- [Gary] And when did you start that?

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.480
- 1985.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:38.995
- [Gary] Keep going.

00:02:38.995 --> 00:02:39.828
- So that's a huge success.

00:02:39.828 --> 00:02:41.095
- [Gary] Yes!

00:02:41.095 --> 00:02:43.660
All worlds by the way would
view that as a success.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:44.820
- Yeah that's what I do professionally.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.160
- [Gary] Understood.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:46.993
Personally.

00:02:49.472 --> 00:02:51.575
- I also do a lot of
stuff with philanthropy,

00:02:51.575 --> 00:02:53.723
which is a long word.

00:02:53.723 --> 00:02:58.723
But basically I give money to
start really unusual things.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.330
I started a program in China,

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.170
called the Schwarzman Scholars.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.695
And what it is, it's like
the Road Scholarship.

00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:11.725
Where we take students
who are extraordinary

00:03:11.725 --> 00:03:16.459
who graduated from university
for graduate degree,

00:03:16.459 --> 00:03:19.060
masters in global affairs.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.020
We bring them to China
to Schwarzman College,

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.780
which is an Oxford
Cambridge type of college,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.530
where the students live there,
they take their meals there,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.135
they have classes there.

00:03:33.135 --> 00:03:38.135
And 40% of the students
are from the United States.

00:03:38.466 --> 00:03:40.220
20% are from China.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.090
And 40% are from other countries.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.908
So basically there are
41 countries right now

00:03:46.908 --> 00:03:48.170
sending students.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.170
- [Gray] When did you start that?

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:53.016
- I started that, came
up with the idea 2011.

00:03:53.016 --> 00:03:58.016
Our first class graduated in 2017.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:01.989
So we have roughly about 150 students now.

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:06.396
Our max capacity as
we're growing it is 200.

00:04:06.396 --> 00:04:09.840
These kids are astonishing.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:10.750
- [Gary] Clearly.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.310
- Really astonishing.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:16.516
We got sponsorship from
the president of China,

00:04:16.516 --> 00:04:20.621
the president of United States,
it's a very unusual thing.

00:04:20.621 --> 00:04:24.211
That takes some of my time.

00:04:24.211 --> 00:04:26.799
But I've also started
some other things around

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.423
artificial intelligence.

00:04:28.423 --> 00:04:32.880
Because I'm concerned
about the impact of A.I.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.504
In terms of disruption in the work force.

00:04:36.504 --> 00:04:38.512
- [Gray] It's foregone conclusion.

00:04:38.512 --> 00:04:41.220
Just like machines for farming.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:42.670
- Well that's also true.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:43.503
- [Gary] Yes, thank you.

00:04:43.503 --> 00:04:46.793
- So I'm looking at that situation.

00:04:46.793 --> 00:04:49.940
By the same token, A.I. can do

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.465
some astonishingly positive things.

00:04:52.465 --> 00:04:57.465
I ended doing a very large donation to MIT

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.690
to start a new college of computing,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:07.440
which on the one hand pushed
the technology forward,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.440
but on the other hand a
place where we can take

00:05:13.163 --> 00:05:16.040
people from around the
world and figure out

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.480
how to introduce this
technology on the basis

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:24.450
where it does the
minimum damage to society

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.940
and people in terms of unemployment.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.950
And I've done something
similar but a little different

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:31.756
at Oxford University.

00:05:31.756 --> 00:05:36.044
There we started another
A.I. ethics thing.

00:05:36.044 --> 00:05:40.124
I gave a big facility for the humanities

00:05:40.124 --> 00:05:45.124
because Oxford interestingly,
that's where it started.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.523
They're number one in the
world as MIT's and science.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.140
Oxford never had their
humanities departments together.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:56.973
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:05:56.973 --> 00:05:58.410
- So this will be the first
time where we'll be building

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:00.083
a big performing arts center.

00:06:01.181 --> 00:06:03.400
We're gonna use humanities to inform that.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.400
Importantly in A.I. because
Oxford got the number one

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.200
philosophy department, ethics
departments in the world.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:16.195
We're gonna use that capability to inform

00:06:16.195 --> 00:06:21.160
how humans react to
artificial intelligence.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:21.993
- [Gary] Couple things.

00:06:21.993 --> 00:06:23.837
Linkedin, I know you
guys are live so please

00:06:23.837 --> 00:06:25.440
fire in your questions.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.761
I know that is very much the
audience for this interview.

00:06:27.761 --> 00:06:30.560
Steph, take it all the way back.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.961
Where were you born and
what kind of kid were you?

00:06:32.961 --> 00:06:35.236
- [Stephen] I was born in Philadelphia.

00:06:35.236 --> 00:06:37.150
And what kind of kid was I?

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:39.280
- Are you a Philly sports fan?

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.853
- I was then but they
lost all the time so.

00:06:41.853 --> 00:06:44.866
I've transferred my
loyalty, I've moved to--

00:06:44.866 --> 00:06:46.640
- [Gary] Where are your current loyalties?

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.436
- I moved to New York in 1969.

00:06:48.436 --> 00:06:49.730
- [Gary] Fair enough.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:51.731
Good year for the Jets and the Mets.

00:06:51.731 --> 00:06:52.747
And the Knicks.

00:06:52.747 --> 00:06:54.800
- Well nobody can beat for the Jets,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.127
it just requires too much
loyalty for too many periods,

00:06:58.127 --> 00:06:59.410
too many decades.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:03.905
- I've not missed a snap of
a New York Jets football game

00:07:03.905 --> 00:07:06.040
since 1982.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.353
- Well then you're a masochist.

00:07:08.353 --> 00:07:09.860
(laughter)

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:11.140
You know, it got too bad--

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:12.454
- [Gary] Or deeply loyal.

00:07:12.454 --> 00:07:14.460
- When they built a new stadium,

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.132
I thought it was really neat to get a box

00:07:18.132 --> 00:07:22.030
and go on a regular basis.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:26.793
Well, it's quite defeating
and so I give the tickets away

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.680
for the most part.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.320
By the end of the season--

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.820
- [Gary] Do you do Jets
and Giants, or just Jets?

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.064
- [Stephen] Both.

00:07:34.064 --> 00:07:35.816
- [Gary] Understood.

00:07:35.816 --> 00:07:37.947
- By the end of the season
nobody would take them.

00:07:37.947 --> 00:07:38.967
- [Gary] I understand.

00:07:38.967 --> 00:07:40.110
Not been the best decade.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.420
- Now that's for free.

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.074
- [Gary] I understand.

00:07:43.074 --> 00:07:43.907
- They won't take them.

00:07:45.115 --> 00:07:46.427
- The market is supply
and demand, my friend.

00:07:46.427 --> 00:07:47.490
(laughter)

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.630
- The only group that
will take them actually,

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.066
none of my people would take them.

00:07:52.066 --> 00:07:54.170
But I give the tickets away to veterans

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.128
and disabled veterans,

00:07:56.128 --> 00:07:58.570
and they take them and have a good time.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:02.270
But I must tell you, you
have to be an optimist

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.040
if you're a Jets fan.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:06.470
- Or you just love the process.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:08.900
I think when I think of
your level of success

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:09.950
or just anybody else.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.750
If one does not love the
process, they have no shot.

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:18.750
And I genuinely, I spent
so much time in control

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:23.300
that it's actually fun
to offset a little bit

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.640
and be super passionate
about something you

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:26.900
don't have control over.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:28.120
Which is why I've decided I'm going to

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.370
buy the New York Jets.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:30.890
Because I want control.

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:32.280
Back to my happiness.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.441
- Well a friend of mine,
Woody Johnson owns the team.

00:08:36.441 --> 00:08:37.320
- [Gary] I'm aware.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.270
- And I try and give him
some advice sometime,

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.819
but apparently I'm not
an effective sales man.

00:08:43.819 --> 00:08:44.899
(laughter)

00:08:44.899 --> 00:08:46.880
- So you grew up in Philly,
what kind of kid where you?

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:47.760
were you a good student,

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.886
were you an entrepreneur
kind of hustler kid,

00:08:49.886 --> 00:08:51.490
what kind of guy were you?

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:55.300
- Well I had two lives.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:09:00.000
The first was in the city until I was 14.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.130
And then the second was in the suburbs.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:07.080
So, in the city I was an athlete

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.010
and I was a good student,

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.894
not at the top of my class
but I tried really hard.

00:09:13.894 --> 00:09:15.539
- Solid.

00:09:15.539 --> 00:09:17.970
- And that's sort of
what you did at that age.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:20.120
- Was that a big conversation
in the household?

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.463
Like, be great at school?

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.200
- [Stephen] Well it was just assumed.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.270
- There was no conversation.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.160
It was a dictatorship.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.180
- It was a culture.

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:30.180
- [Gary] Understood.

00:09:31.113 --> 00:09:32.360
- Of do well.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:33.962
- And where were you in the lineage,

00:09:33.962 --> 00:09:34.971
were you the oldest?

00:09:34.971 --> 00:09:35.985
- I was the oldest.

00:09:35.985 --> 00:09:36.984
- [Gary] So even more so.

00:09:36.984 --> 00:09:38.670
- Yeah you have a little
bit of pressure on that.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:43.150
But I always remember being outside

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:46.342
and competing at something.

00:09:46.342 --> 00:09:49.020
I just loved sports.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:53.973
I moved to the suburbs and
it wasn't as difficult.

00:09:53.973 --> 00:09:58.383
I grew up in an an area
with broken bottles all over

00:09:58.383 --> 00:10:02.350
the playgrounds and if you
played soccer against somebody,

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:03.816
something bad could happen to you.

00:10:03.816 --> 00:10:05.465
- Do you feel thought those
first 14 years in that

00:10:05.465 --> 00:10:10.465
kind of environment gave
you some real advantages

00:10:10.845 --> 00:10:15.320
form of grit and kid
of E.Q. kind of sense?

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:16.280
- Absolutely.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:19.310
If I hadn't been raised
in a place like that,

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:20.803
I wouldn't understand.

00:10:21.905 --> 00:10:25.200
I wouldn't have understood evil,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.323
I wouldn't have understood bad behavior.

00:10:28.323 --> 00:10:32.316
That bad things could
happen to nice people.

00:10:32.316 --> 00:10:35.910
One of my closest friend when I was young,

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:40.910
his father was assassinated
when we were about 10

00:10:41.107 --> 00:10:45.650
in some motel, I don't know
what this guy was doing

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:50.650
but my friend had a box
completely filled with money.

00:10:52.918 --> 00:10:57.210
I wasn't even given money
so I was in a neighborhood

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:02.073
that was somewhat, I guess
you can call it challenged.

00:11:03.214 --> 00:11:05.975
So, that was, my mother--

00:11:05.975 --> 00:11:09.144
- And were your parents born here,

00:11:09.144 --> 00:11:10.436
or did they emigrate--

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:11.812
- [Stephen] Excuse me?

00:11:11.812 --> 00:11:12.941
- Were your parents born here in the U.S.?

00:11:12.941 --> 00:11:14.367
- Yeah.

00:11:14.367 --> 00:11:15.374
- And before that, who was--

00:11:15.374 --> 00:11:18.440
- [Stephen] My great grandfather
came over from Austria

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.947
on one side of the
family and the other side

00:11:20.947 --> 00:11:24.770
was from Ukraine, 1880's.

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:25.643
- [Gary] Understood.

00:11:28.403 --> 00:11:31.399
- So I moved to the suburbs,
things have changed.

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:33.653
Everybody was lovely.

00:11:36.136 --> 00:11:37.750
You know, I became president of the school

00:11:38.867 --> 00:11:40.290
and the top athlete.

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.123
- Life was good.

00:11:41.123 --> 00:11:45.610
- Life was astonishingly good,

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:48.090
unsuspectingly good.

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:52.545
And has more or less remained so.

00:11:52.545 --> 00:11:57.545
And if my mother hadn't
beaten my dad up to move

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.970
'cause he didn't want to move

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:06.470
and get us in a better school district.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:08.832
She just did it for the school district.

00:12:08.832 --> 00:12:12.660
Then I wouldn't be sitting here with you.

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.123
- What drew you to finance?

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.410
- Well that was an accident,

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:20.510
I didn't know anything about finance.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:25.510
In fact I got hired when
I graduated from college.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:27.950
I didn't even have a job.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:29.700
- What did you think you were
gonna do in those college

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.120
years after school?

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.120
- I had actually, surprisingly no idea.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:37.293
- Why?

00:12:38.141 --> 00:12:40.150
Were you so focused on the
enjoyment of those four years,

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:42.679
did you have so many interests,
you were crippled by that,

00:12:42.679 --> 00:12:45.040
did you have a main interest

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:47.360
that faded towards the end of it?

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.271
What was the variable there?

00:12:49.271 --> 00:12:51.892
- I mean, I was interested in university.

00:12:51.892 --> 00:12:53.898
In learning how to think,

00:12:53.898 --> 00:12:55.679
excelling,

00:12:55.679 --> 00:13:00.679
I wasn't a joiner of organizations.

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:06.270
My first English paper,

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:11.270
I got a 68% on "Bartleby, the
Scrivener" by Herman Melville.

00:13:12.304 --> 00:13:17.304
My second English paper I got a 66%.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.520
This was a trend even I could recognize

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:23.657
was not so good.

00:13:23.657 --> 00:13:26.918
And if I continued would result in--

00:13:26.918 --> 00:13:28.366
- [Gary] Demise.

00:13:28.366 --> 00:13:30.731
- In my not being university.

00:13:30.731 --> 00:13:35.731
So I fortunately had a teacher,

00:13:35.877 --> 00:13:37.811
called me in for a meeting

00:13:37.811 --> 00:13:39.510
and he said Mr. Schwarzman,

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:43.360
we're here to talk about
your English papers.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:46.265
I said why would we do that?

00:13:46.265 --> 00:13:47.846
(laughter)

00:13:47.846 --> 00:13:52.846
I said there's really no reason for me.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:59.677
And he said, I don't understand
why you would think that.

00:13:59.677 --> 00:14:04.677
I said because I had nothing
to say and I said it poorly

00:14:06.207 --> 00:14:09.650
in those papers.

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:12.896
And he said, my god you're not stupid!

00:14:12.896 --> 00:14:17.896
He said you just can't
think and you can't write.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:22.110
He said, so I can't teach
you both at the same time.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:26.283
So I'll start off
teaching you how to write.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:30.971
I'll give you the answers to the essays

00:14:30.971 --> 00:14:33.050
and will teach you the answers to write,

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:35.560
'cause you can't think and
write at the same time.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.455
He said, then after you learn how to write

00:14:38.455 --> 00:14:39.403
I'll teach you how to think.

00:14:40.304 --> 00:14:41.810
So I ended up on Dean's
list, I went to Yale

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:43.377
so it wasn't like a bad school.

00:14:43.377 --> 00:14:48.377
I ended up on Dean's list which
is the top 10% of the class

00:14:49.412 --> 00:14:51.463
by the end of the first year.

00:14:52.465 --> 00:14:55.010
So, I was interested in
sort of learning things.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:57.200
- [Gary] Steph, what do you
think of the current state of

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.124
how we package and process education?

00:15:00.124 --> 00:15:05.124
- [Stephen] I think education
is not doing a good job.

00:15:08.092 --> 00:15:09.260
- [Gary] No shit.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:12.351
- For the people it's intended to help.

00:15:12.351 --> 00:15:17.351
There's probably a lot
of reasons for that.

00:15:17.696 --> 00:15:20.774
Whether it's primary or secondary.

00:15:20.774 --> 00:15:23.013
University is different.

00:15:23.013 --> 00:15:25.313
We have some great universities,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.190
so that's not a broken system in the way

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:30.450
primary, secondary.

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:32.960
- At the level of scale that you play with

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:35.310
the companies that you get involved with,

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:37.620
at the P.E. level or the hedge fund level.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:42.530
At that scale, I'm sure you
recognize connection point

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.110
between large organizations
in the business world

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:47.940
and large organizations in
the environment of education.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.040
- [Stephen] Absolutely.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:52.630
- It is stunning to me for
what I do for a living,

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:56.854
the complete inability for
the fortune 500 landscape

00:15:56.854 --> 00:16:00.923
to have adjusted to the
communications infrastructure

00:16:00.923 --> 00:16:03.984
that the internet at this
maturity level has created.

00:16:03.984 --> 00:16:07.678
In your world, I do not believe the vast

00:16:07.678 --> 00:16:12.470
majority of your world yet
has recognized the stunning

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:15.720
amount of money that big
brands waste everyday

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:16.670
on communication.

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:20.810
And I'm talking to the delta
on the 90 cents on the dollar.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:23.236
I often say, once the
activist investors actually

00:16:23.236 --> 00:16:24.703
understand, forget about printing
on both sides of the paper

00:16:24.703 --> 00:16:29.030
or travel, or cutting overhead.

00:16:29.030 --> 00:16:32.500
That the sheer money that the fortune 500

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.780
wastes everyday on marketing.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:36.930
It's gonna get really interesting.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:41.025
It's been the inability of
Madison Avenue, Wall Street,

00:16:41.025 --> 00:16:43.630
and the corporate landscape
to adjust to the sheer

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:45.440
speed of consumer behavior.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:47.641
There's a complete compt to
the same thing on education.

00:16:47.641 --> 00:16:51.380
We continue to teach
kids to memorize stuff

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:54.420
when the information is
literally on their phone.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:56.803
- Well, as to the corporate world.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:01.300
You usually have a generational issue

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:04.450
where the people in
charge aren't the people

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:08.195
who've learned the
breakthrough technologies

00:17:08.195 --> 00:17:10.996
that have happened over the last years.

00:17:10.996 --> 00:17:12.170
- [Gary] That's correct.

00:17:12.170 --> 00:17:15.690
Or Steph, the compensation
that that leadership has

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:18.699
doesn't, the infrastructure
of short term numbers

00:17:18.699 --> 00:17:23.003
and being able to cash out
within two or three years

00:17:23.003 --> 00:17:24.560
when you're out of the position

00:17:25.576 --> 00:17:26.940
even though you've only been
there for two to three years.

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:30.060
Doesn't necessarily put
CEO's in today's landscape

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:32.500
in a position to actually
care about the health

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:35.000
of a business a decade from today.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:36.370
- [Stephen] Well that
one I'll fight you on.

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:37.203
- Please.

00:17:37.203 --> 00:17:39.625
- Because the person
you see in that position

00:17:39.625 --> 00:17:42.948
has come up through a
variety of other positions.

00:17:42.948 --> 00:17:46.050
And people who spend
their life doing something

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.583
aren't necessarily all
short term oriented.

00:17:49.583 --> 00:17:51.550
- [Gary] No they're not.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.150
- So they want their organization,

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.810
just like you want you
podcast to be a winner.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:59.640
- Well let's play that a
little bit differently.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.290
It's very different to be an executive

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.645
who spent within the McDonald's Inc

00:18:03.645 --> 00:18:07.230
to then become the CMO but then transfer

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.481
and go work at 3G on Burger King,

00:18:09.481 --> 00:18:12.240
is quite different than
me being in perpetuity

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:13.400
with my personal brand.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:15.928
- Well I'm in perpetuity
with my personal brand

00:18:15.928 --> 00:18:17.050
at Blackstone.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:19.424
- [Gary] Sure, but we're
not talking about you.

00:18:19.424 --> 00:18:20.654
- [Stephen] I understand.

00:18:20.654 --> 00:18:22.206
- We're talking about
executives that run companies

00:18:22.206 --> 00:18:24.730
who transact on stock price,

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:27.910
who also can spend 15 to
20 years to get to that

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:29.970
point where they're
really making economics.

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:31.680
By the way, you'll appreciate this.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.610
I don't blame that, that's human behavior.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:34.890
I don't think it's a negative.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:37.300
But I think it's a really
interesting dynamic.

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:39.153
- [Stephen] That's a separate subject

00:18:39.153 --> 00:18:44.153
than introduction and
effectiveness of technology

00:18:44.334 --> 00:18:48.290
for large scale organization.

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:50.040
- Real quick, I apologize,

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.261
it is "Interrupted By."

00:18:51.261 --> 00:18:52.423
- [Stephen] No problem.

00:18:52.423 --> 00:18:53.900
- You'll appreciate this.

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:56.320
I think it's fascinating dynamic.

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:57.430
Yes.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:19:00.770
But it's also the reason, some, not all,

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:02.470
this is always a some game right.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:05.140
Struggle with taking
the one step backwards

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:09.210
to make the capital investments
to have the patience

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:13.472
around consumer behavior
in lieu of milking out

00:19:13.472 --> 00:19:16.103
the infrastructure cost they invested in

00:19:16.103 --> 00:19:18.121
within a half decade period.

00:19:18.121 --> 00:19:21.697
- I think it's to the
extent that that's true,

00:19:21.697 --> 00:19:24.002
but I don't think it's
an economic decision.

00:19:24.002 --> 00:19:26.292
I think it's a lack of familiarity.

00:19:26.292 --> 00:19:31.292
If people understood
how profoundly they can

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:38.451
penetrate the market or be effective,

00:19:38.451 --> 00:19:39.830
they would do it.

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:40.940
- Steve--

00:19:40.940 --> 00:19:43.670
- Because the other problem
is if you're not doing it,

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:45.663
somebody else is.

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:48.950
You're gonna be severely damaged.

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:51.433
So there is some rough frontier justice.

00:19:51.433 --> 00:19:54.235
- It's a game of where is the balance.

00:19:54.235 --> 00:19:57.530
Subconscious let alone the
conscious of the individual

00:19:57.530 --> 00:20:01.346
that's leading on their balance
between legacy and currency.

00:20:01.346 --> 00:20:03.363
- Well also, comfort.

00:20:03.363 --> 00:20:04.881
In other words--

00:20:04.881 --> 00:20:06.158
- [Gary] No that's very fair.

00:20:06.158 --> 00:20:09.740
Because I think an
innovating entrepreneur,

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:12.603
she or he has on comfort in chaos

00:20:12.603 --> 00:20:15.458
whereas a type A operator,

00:20:15.458 --> 00:20:18.990
his only comfort is in
very different dynamic.

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:19.823
- Yeah.

00:20:21.841 --> 00:20:24.041
Entrepreneurs don't
believe they're in chaos

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:28.080
you may think so but they don't.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:32.314
The reason being an entrepreneur yourself

00:20:32.314 --> 00:20:36.010
of a certain type or myself,

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.979
you only make decisions
when you feel you're right.

00:20:38.979 --> 00:20:39.812
- 100%.

00:20:39.812 --> 00:20:43.800
- You don't make decisions to take risk.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:45.174
- [Gary] I couldn't agree with you more.

00:20:45.174 --> 00:20:47.840
- The idea that entrepreneurs are risk

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:49.871
takers is complete bs.

00:20:49.871 --> 00:20:53.070
- I'm a buyer, that's bullshit

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.921
for people that watch this show.

00:20:54.921 --> 00:20:57.520
- They're risk takers
from the perspectives

00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:59.910
of people who are watching them.

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:02.320
Not from them themselves.

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:05.581
- We think it's the reverse.

00:21:05.581 --> 00:21:07.730
We think the person that's going this way

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:09.040
is actually going wrong.

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:13.350
- [Stephen] Right and so
people who are entrepreneurs

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:17.358
just see the same facts differently.

00:21:17.358 --> 00:21:20.116
Or they actually obtain different facts.

00:21:20.116 --> 00:21:24.530
And those different
facts show them something

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:26.200
that's wholly logical.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:30.108
In my own case, I can
explain everything I do

00:21:30.108 --> 00:21:35.108
exactly why I do it and
I'm also totally open

00:21:35.233 --> 00:21:40.233
to someone proving that that's incorrect.

00:21:40.430 --> 00:21:42.420
Because I don't want to be wrong.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:47.206
But when I see it and if other people--

00:21:47.206 --> 00:21:48.456
- [Gary] Don't.

00:21:50.286 --> 00:21:51.378
- Sometimes you know what's interesting?

00:21:51.378 --> 00:21:52.530
They see it but they don't want to change

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:53.670
what they're doing.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:57.779
It's the nature of the
human being I found out.

00:21:57.779 --> 00:21:58.870
- [Gary] Couldn't agree more.

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:03.740
- Because I'm not 25, so I
had a lot of observations

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:05.463
of the human condition.

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:09.460
Even with a wonderfully
conceived new thing,

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:10.960
if you describe it to people,

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:13.474
hardly anyone else would follow

00:22:13.474 --> 00:22:17.070
because they're comfortable
doing whatever they're doing--

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:19.352
- [Gary] It goes back
to the point earlier,

00:22:19.352 --> 00:22:20.185
the executives running these companies

00:22:20.185 --> 00:22:21.330
in a lot of ways, in my opinion

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:25.890
I believe that the
discomfort and then look,

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:30.160
humans are game of vested
interest in the subconscious

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:31.400
and conscious, it's what it is.

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:33.339
It's an animalistic game.

00:22:33.339 --> 00:22:36.922
- I don't quite understand it fully.

00:22:36.922 --> 00:22:40.636
I'm grateful it exists because it's helped

00:22:40.636 --> 00:22:43.986
my little corner of the world.

00:22:43.986 --> 00:22:44.980
- I'm sorry to interrupt,

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:47.709
in this perspective of watching operators

00:22:47.709 --> 00:22:52.415
give me a couple of your
favorite operators in the balance

00:22:52.415 --> 00:22:55.210
of let's call, let's sum
up what we're talking about

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:56.460
right now, the balance of black and white,

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:58.182
versus the gray of
operations and strategy.

00:22:58.182 --> 00:23:01.640
Who stood out?

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:02.860
Some of the investments you've made

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:04.720
where you watched from
afar that have really

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.400
as you've gotten closer and watched,

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:09.250
you've just gotten fond of
or unique or interesting?

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:14.250
- Well, I actually don't
encounter people in that way

00:23:17.259 --> 00:23:19.835
and think about them.

00:23:19.835 --> 00:23:24.835
When I was younger there
was a guy operating

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:30.910
within that now obsolete
world who was the head of

00:23:31.785 --> 00:23:35.414
GE named Jack Welsh.

00:23:35.414 --> 00:23:40.414
He looked at everything as
if it was completely new.

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:43.882
He listened to everyone intensely.

00:23:43.882 --> 00:23:48.882
He had this unique ability,
still does, he's still alive

00:23:50.208 --> 00:23:55.208
to interrogate somebody and
learn everything they knew

00:23:56.806 --> 00:24:00.391
in the shortest period
of time I have ever seen.

00:24:00.391 --> 00:24:04.560
And then he'd take that on
board and to that with a

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:08.853
succession of people and
then come to a conclusion

00:24:08.853 --> 00:24:13.400
that an organization was doing
something right or wrong,

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:16.530
could do better, should
go in a different thing.

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:20.478
And what he would do was
challenge and push them

00:24:20.478 --> 00:24:22.170
to do that.

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:27.170
And he would go wherever
those insights were.

00:24:27.170 --> 00:24:32.050
This is a completely
courageous person with a

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:36.670
giant brain, endless energy,

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:41.670
and took that company into
the largest market value

00:24:43.068 --> 00:24:48.068
because of just the extraordinarily
nature of the person.

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:51.528
- [Gary] Of course, no question.

00:24:51.528 --> 00:24:56.528
- I met him before he
had his job at the top,

00:24:56.540 --> 00:24:59.470
and I was asked by the head of

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:02.469
his company to teach him finance.

00:25:02.469 --> 00:25:07.469
That took like two days and
a limited number of hours

00:25:10.731 --> 00:25:15.040
and he could suck out of my
brain anything I have in there.

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:17.492
Even stuff I didn't know and off he went.

00:25:17.492 --> 00:25:20.109
And occasionally you run into these

00:25:20.109 --> 00:25:24.190
extraordinary human beings.

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:25.070
- Why'd you write the book?

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:27.593
- [Stephen] I wrote the book because,

00:25:28.479 --> 00:25:33.479
starting about 7 to 10 years ago,

00:25:36.250 --> 00:25:39.930
people instead of my going and

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:41.843
selling them products that we had,

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.220
I'd meet somebody at the top,

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:48.220
particularly there was an
incident in the Middle East

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:51.700
with the head of a very
large Sovereign Wealth Fund,

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:54.207
which is savings of their country.

00:25:54.207 --> 00:25:59.207
He was new and I had an introduction,

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:05.660
I was the first person he
was gonna meet outside of

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:09.432
his world before he was appointed.

00:26:09.432 --> 00:26:13.068
And instead of a five minute handshake,

00:26:13.068 --> 00:26:17.526
he wanted to know how to
run his organization better

00:26:17.526 --> 00:26:19.850
because he wasn't experienced.

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:21.944
So I ended up spending
two and a half hours

00:26:21.944 --> 00:26:24.078
telling him how to do stuff.

00:26:24.078 --> 00:26:26.878
So for me it's pretty
easy, it's what I do.

00:26:26.878 --> 00:26:31.563
- [Gary] It's what you do, I get it.

00:26:32.517 --> 00:26:34.080
- I certainly could do that.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:37.801
That surprised everybody
because that's not

00:26:37.801 --> 00:26:40.726
what's supposed to happen.

00:26:40.726 --> 00:26:42.590
And then when I was
going around the world,

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:47.590
which I do meeting groups
of people who give us money.

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:52.820
All of the sudden, nobody wanted
to hear about our products.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:56.300
And this particular guy,
the first guy was very funny

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:57.770
I tried to sell him something

00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:01.297
in the middle of showing him
what he ought to be doing,

00:27:01.297 --> 00:27:04.920
he said, please Mr. Schwarzman
don't waste my time.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:06.460
(laughter)

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:09.313
He said, we'll buy your products anyhow.

00:27:09.313 --> 00:27:12.270
Your time is too precious
for me, I want to--

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:14.329
- [Gary] To be on the sales
pitch, I totally understand.

00:27:14.329 --> 00:27:18.214
So that became the seed of
there's something there?

00:27:18.214 --> 00:27:21.058
- Yeah and what happened
was that started happening

00:27:21.058 --> 00:27:22.903
everywhere I went.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:28.970
And then I realized that I was
spending two and a half hours

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:31.330
every time I was seeing somebody.

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.736
And if I wrote it down, then I could--

00:27:34.736 --> 00:27:35.811
- [Gary] You could save some time?

00:27:35.811 --> 00:27:37.292
- Then I could just give them the book.

00:27:37.292 --> 00:27:39.088
(laughter)

00:27:39.088 --> 00:27:40.169
It would save me the time.

00:27:40.169 --> 00:27:41.372
- So it's a time arbitrage?

00:27:41.372 --> 00:27:42.420
- Right that's what it was.

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:43.756
- [Gary] I love that.

00:27:43.756 --> 00:27:45.210
